# R Markdown Notes  

## Basically some tricks  
So in markdown, gotta remember that an asterisk does *this* to words, and two  
asterisks do **this** to words. Idk how to do underlining yet.  

Also, really need to remember about double  spacing for new lines, and then  
double entering for a new paragraph (with a space in between).  

### Obviously Headers too  
Many type of headers, shown by hashtags, more = smaller.  
Still, useful to know.  

### Hyperlinks  
Yeah you betcha, you can add hyperlinks like so:  
<https://www.google.com>  
Just add the less than/greater than signs on it and you're good to go.  
Also there's this other way to do links:  
[Google](https://www.google.com)  
That way works by having the hyperlink text in square brackets, and the  
hyperlink itself in parentheses right beside it.  

*remember that it only works if you open this html knit on your browser.*

### Bullet Points
Yep, bullet points too. I don't know if there's more options, but as far as  
I know you get them by using asterisks.  
* How?  
Just like that. So you type in an asterisk followed by a space and your words.

## Code handling and such  
Very important, since that's the bread and butter of R Markdown.  
**STILL** you need to keep it in mind.  

Right now I've got this file set up to show code, so it looks like this:  
```{r}
library(tidyverse)
library(ggplot2)
library(palmerpenguins)
head(penguins)
```

when I do something in R.  

### What shortcut for adding code?  
It's Ctrl+Alt+I, so yeah remember that super helpful. Just remember that any  
words after r and before the comma in the curly brackets is the name of the  
code segment.  

### How to hide/unhide code in RMD  
So basically since there's cases when it can be useful to show the code,
I need to know when to do so. So there's two options I've gotten so far,
and they are this:

* **Option 1:** I set up the file in the beginning with this stuff:
[knitr::opts_chunk$set(echo = FALSE)]
which will turn the code off for good, but idk how to turn it back on.  
* **Option 2:** I set up in each code segment in the curly brackets this:
{r, echo = FALSE, warning = FALSE, message = FALSE}
and it would work on ones I want to hide vs ones I don't, like so:
```{r penguins, echo=FALSE, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE}
ggplot(data = penguins, mapping = aes(x =flipper_length_mm, y = body_mass_g)) +
  geom_point(mapping = aes(color = island, shape = species)) +
  labs(title = "Penguin Flipper Length compared to Body Mass",
       caption = "Data Collected by Dr. Kristen Gorman from 2007-2009",
       x = "Body Mass (g)", y = "Flipper Length  (mm)") +
  geom_smooth(method = "loess") +
  facet_wrap(~sex)
```

and voila I made a pretty good diagram without showing all the R code to make
it, and if I wanted to show it I just don't include that echo segment.  

### How to add images
So we're adding pictures now? Yep, and how you do it is pretty similar to how  
you add hyperlinks with text, just put an exclamation mark in front, followed  
by the caption of the image in brackets and the link to the image in parentheses.  

So it should look like this:  
![Map of Tamriel](https://www.imperial-library.info/wp-content/uploads/image-2.png)
